For the UrQMD part:

In order to run the full analysis, you need to have simulation data from UrQMD. The data used in this analysis was generated using version 3.4 of UrQMD, which can be downloaded e.g. from this fork: https://github.com/sh1r4s3/urqmd
The simulation can be run using the runall.bash script in the urqmd-3.4 directory. Keep in mind that the full simulation would consume months of CPU time on a single core. 

After simulation data is ready, in order to run the full analysis:

time ./do_all_urqmd.sh 1 &> allurqmd.log &

Also keep in mind that the analysis might consume cca. 20 GB of RAM at its peak even on a single core (for 100k events). Before running the analysis, study carefully the comments in the beginning of the do_all_urqmd.sh script.

BEWARE! Even on 3 threads, the full analysis will run for cca. 770 minutes on 100 000 events, the fitting process (measured with the FFT method) 140 minutes.

# The EPOS stuff is already deprecated, if datafiles are available, 
